('leadership',
 'Tell me about a time you led a cross-functional team to deliver a complex project',
 'As TPM at Amazon, I led a 15-person team across 4 organizations (Payments, Fraud, Mobile, Data Science) to build a new checkout flow for Prime Day 2024. The project had a hard deadline of June 1st and required coordinating 12 different services. I established weekly cross-team syncs, created a shared Gantt chart tracking 47 dependencies, and implemented a risk register updated daily. When the Mobile team fell 2 weeks behind due to iOS framework issues, I negotiated with leadership to shift 2 engineers from another team and ran daily standups to unblock issues. We launched 3 days early with 99.9 percent uptime during Prime Day, processing 2.3M transactions with 15 percent faster checkout time and zero P0 incidents.',
 'As TPM at Amazon, I led a 15-person team across 4 organizations (Payments, Fraud, Mobile, Data Science) to build a new checkout flow for Prime Day 2024. The project had a hard deadline of June 1st and required coordinating 12 different services.',
 'My task was to deliver the new checkout flow on time while coordinating 4 different teams with competing priorities, managing 47 cross-service dependencies, and ensuring zero impact to existing Prime Day traffic.',
 'I established weekly cross-team syncs with all 4 organizations, created a shared Gantt chart tracking all 47 dependencies in JIRA, and implemented a risk register that I updated daily. When the Mobile team fell 2 weeks behind due to iOS framework issues, I negotiated with leadership to temporarily shift 2 engineers from another project and ran daily 15-minute standups focused purely on unblocking critical path items.',
 'We launched 3 days ahead of schedule with 99.9 percent uptime during Prime Day. The new checkout flow processed 2.3 million transactions, achieved 15 percent faster completion time compared to the old flow, and had zero P0 incidents. Leadership recognized the project as a model for cross-org collaboration.',
 5, 5, 5, 5, 5.0, 'senior', true),

('leadership',
 'Describe a situation where you had to influence a decision without having direct authority',
 'At Google, I was TPM for infrastructure reliability but had no authority over the SRE team. They wanted to maintain 5 different monitoring tools which created 40 percent of our on-call burden due to alert noise. I created a cost analysis showing we spent 2.4M dollars annually on monitoring tools plus 800 engineer-hours monthly on alert triage. I organized lunch-and-learns where I invited the SRE leads to share pain points, then presented data showing 78 percent of alerts were duplicates. I proposed a phased migration to a unified stack, starting with a 2-team pilot. After the pilot reduced their on-call burden by 60 percent, I secured buy-in from 8 other teams. Within 6 months, we consolidated to 2 tools, reducing costs by 1.6M dollars per year and alert fatigue by 71 percent.',
 'At Google, I was TPM for infrastructure reliability but had no authority over the SRE team. They maintained 5 different monitoring tools which created significant operational overhead, but they resisted consolidation.',
 'I needed to convince the SRE team to consolidate monitoring tools without having any direct authority over them, while they were initially resistant to change due to concerns about losing flexibility.',
 'I created a comprehensive cost analysis showing 2.4M dollars annual spend and 800 engineer-hours monthly on alert triage. I organized lunch-and-learn sessions where SRE leads could voice concerns, then presented data showing 78 percent of alerts were duplicates. Instead of mandating change, I proposed a low-risk 2-team pilot program and personally supported the migration.',
 'After the pilot reduced on-call burden by 60 percent, I secured buy-in from 8 additional SRE teams. Within 6 months, we consolidated from 5 tools to 2, reducing monitoring costs by 1.6M dollars annually and decreasing alert fatigue by 71 percent. The SRE director later credited this as a model for data-driven influence.',
 5, 5, 5, 5, 5.0, 'senior', true),

('conflict',
 'Tell me about a time you resolved a conflict with a key stakeholder',
 'As TPM at Meta, I managed a data pipeline project where the Product Manager wanted to launch in 6 weeks to hit Q2 goals, but the Tech Lead insisted we needed 12 weeks for proper data quality validation. The PM threatened to escalate to my VP. I facilitated a 2-hour working session where both parties documented their constraints and risks. I proposed a phased approach: launch core features in 6 weeks with manual quality checks, then automate validation over the following 6 weeks. I created a detailed risk matrix showing the PM we could hit 80 percent of metrics in phase 1, and showed the Tech Lead we would have automated safeguards before scaling to 100M users. Both agreed. We launched on time, the PM hit Q2 goals with 78 percent of target metrics, and by week 10 we had full automation with zero data quality incidents.',
 'As TPM at Meta, the Product Manager wanted to launch a data pipeline in 6 weeks to hit Q2 goals, but the Tech Lead insisted on 12 weeks for proper data quality validation. The PM was threatening to escalate to my VP.',
 'I needed to find a solution that satisfied both the PM business timeline and the Tech Lead quality requirements, while maintaining team relationships and avoiding executive escalation.',
 'I facilitated a 2-hour working session where both parties documented their constraints and risks. I proposed a phased approach: launch core features in 6 weeks with manual quality checks, then automate validation over the following 6 weeks. I created a detailed risk matrix showing we could achieve 80 percent of metrics in phase 1 while building toward full automation.',
 'Both stakeholders agreed to the phased approach. We launched on time, hitting 78 percent of the PM target metrics in phase 1, and by week 10 we had full automation in place. The next quarter saw zero data quality incidents while processing 100M users. Both the PM and Tech Lead later cited this as an example of collaborative problem-solving.',
 5, 5, 5, 5, 5.0, 'senior', true),

('conflict',
 'Describe a time when you had to resolve a technical disagreement between engineering teams',
 'At Microsoft, two engineering teams disagreed on API design. Backend wanted REST, Frontend wanted GraphQL. The debate stalled the project for 3 weeks. I organized a structured workshop where each team presented data: Backend showed REST could reuse 60 percent of existing auth middleware, Frontend demonstrated GraphQL would reduce API calls from 5 to 1. I created a scorecard evaluating both across 6 dimensions. The data showed GraphQL scored higher on 4 out of 6. I proposed GraphQL with a Backend-owned BFF layer, letting Backend team own the graph schema design. Both teams agreed, we shipped 2 weeks later, and page load times improved by 35 percent.',
 'At Microsoft, Backend and Frontend teams disagreed on API design. Backend wanted REST, Frontend wanted GraphQL. The disagreement stalled the project for 3 weeks.',
 'I needed to break the deadlock, make a data-driven decision that both teams would accept, and ensure we could ship without further delays while maintaining team morale.',
 'I organized a structured workshop where each team presented quantitative data. I created a scorecard evaluating both options across 6 dimensions. When data showed GraphQL scored higher on 4 out of 6, I proposed a compromise: GraphQL with a Backend-owned BFF layer, allowing Backend to leverage their expertise.',
 'Both teams agreed to the hybrid approach. We shipped 2 weeks later. Page load times improved by 35 percent, API calls reduced from 5 to 1 per page, and Backend successfully owned the graph schema design. The Tech Director later used this as a case study for data-driven decisions.',
 5, 5, 5, 5, 5.0, 'senior', true),

('failure',
 'Tell me about a time you failed on a project and what you learned',
 'At Amazon, I was TPM for a machine learning recommendation engine expected to increase conversion by 10 percent. After 6 months, we launched and conversion dropped by 2 percent. I had failed to establish proper A/B testing - we only tested the full experience, not components. The ML model was great (12 percent lift), but the UI was confusing users (negative 14 percent impact). I took full ownership in front of my VP and 50 stakeholders. I halted the rollout, created a detailed test plan with 8 separate experiments, and established weekly metrics reviews. We relaunched 8 weeks later with refined UI. This time we saw 15 percent conversion lift. I documented lessons learned in a runbook that became required reading for all TPMs. The VP later promoted me, citing my ownership and learning from failure.',
 'At Amazon, I was TPM for a machine learning recommendation engine expected to increase conversion by 10 percent. After 6 months of development, we launched and conversion actually dropped by 2 percent instead of increasing.',
 'I needed to understand why the launch failed, take ownership of the mistake, fix the problem quickly, and ensure this type of failure did not happen again across the organization.',
 'I immediately halted the rollout and conducted a thorough post-mortem. The ML model was strong (positive 12 percent lift) but the UI was confusing (negative 14 percent impact). I took full ownership in front of my VP and 50 stakeholders, created a detailed 8-part test plan, and personally led the redesign with feature flags for gradual rollout.',
 'We relaunched 8 weeks later, achieving 15 percent conversion lift (exceeding original goals). I documented lessons learned in a runbook that became required reading for all TPMs. The VP later cited my ownership and learning from failure as a key reason for my promotion.',
 5, 5, 5, 5, 5.0, 'senior', true),

('failure',
 'Describe a time you missed a critical deadline and how you handled it',
 'At Google, I was TPM for a GDPR privacy compliance project with legal deadline March 1st. We missed it by 11 days due to my underestimating data migration complexity - I allocated 3 weeks but it took 6. This exposed the company to potential fines. I immediately informed my director and legal, taking full responsibility. I created a crash plan: joined on-call rotation 24/7 to unblock engineers, negotiated with legal to implement compensating controls (manual processes) while automation was built, and brought in 3 contractors. I created a post-mortem and implemented a new process requiring technical validation on timeline estimates over 2 weeks. We completed March 12th, legal confirmed no fines were incurred, and my new estimation process reduced project delays by 40 percent org-wide.',
 'At Google, I was TPM for a GDPR privacy compliance project with legal deadline March 1st. We missed the deadline by 11 days because I had underestimated data migration complexity (allocated 3 weeks, actually took 6), exposing the company to potential regulatory fines.',
 'I needed to immediately mitigate legal risk, complete the migration as quickly as possible, take responsibility for the miss, and implement processes to prevent future estimation failures.',
 'I immediately informed my director and legal team, taking full personal responsibility. I created a crash plan where I joined the on-call rotation 24/7, negotiated compensating controls with legal, brought in 3 contractors, and conducted a thorough post-mortem. I implemented a new process requiring technical validation on all timeline estimates over 2 weeks.',
 'We completed the migration March 12th. Legal confirmed no fines were incurred due to the compensating controls. The new estimation process I implemented reduced project delays by 40 percent across the organization over the next year. My director praised my transparency and proactive crisis management.',
 5, 5, 5, 5, 5.0, 'senior', true),

('program_sense',
 'Tell me about the most complex program you managed',
 'At Meta, I managed migrating 500 microservices from EC2 to Kubernetes over 18 months, impacting 2000 engineers across 40 teams. The challenge: 30 percent of services had circular dependencies. I built a dependency graph with 1200 edges, used topological sorting to create a 6-wave migration plan. I established weekly steering committee with cross-functional leadership, required dry-run migrations 2 weeks before cutover, created live dashboard tracking 47 metrics. When Security discovered hardcoded credentials during dry-runs, I paused wave 3, ran credentials rotation sprint, added automated scanning. We completed 2 weeks early, achieved 12M dollars annual savings, reduced deployment times by 60 percent, had only 3 incidents across 500 services.',
 'At Meta, I managed migrating 500 microservices from EC2 to Kubernetes over 18 months, impacting 2000 engineers across 40 teams. The challenge was 30 percent of services had circular dependencies.',
 'I needed to sequence 500 service migrations while resolving circular dependencies, coordinate 40 teams, manage risk across 18 months, and achieve cost and performance targets without major production incidents.',
 'I built a dependency graph with 1200 edges and used topological sorting to create a 6-wave migration plan. I established weekly steering committee, required dry-run migrations 2 weeks before cutover, created live dashboard tracking 47 metrics. When Security found hardcoded credentials, I paused wave 3, ran rotation sprint, added automated scanning.',
 'We completed 2 weeks early, achieved 12M dollars annual savings, reduced deployment times by 60 percent, had only 3 production incidents across 500 services (99.4 percent success rate). The dependency analysis framework became the standard for all large-scale migrations at Meta.',
 5, 5, 5, 5, 5.0, 'senior', true),

('program_sense',
 'Describe how you prioritized competing high-priority initiatives',
 'At Amazon, I was TPM for both Prime Day feature (hard deadline, 50M dollars revenue) and critical security vulnerability (potential data breach). Both needed the same 8 engineers. I created a decision framework: quantified financial risk (50M dollars vs 200M dollars plus potential breach), assessed timeline flexibility, evaluated resource dependencies. I proposed: 5 engineers on Prime Day, 3 on security, plus 400K dollars security consulting firm for remaining work - expensive but justified. I reviewed both projects daily. Prime Day launched on time with all features, security vulnerabilities patched 5 days early. My director rolled out the prioritization framework org-wide.',
 'At Amazon, I was TPM for both Prime Day feature (hard deadline, 50M dollars revenue impact) and critical security vulnerability (potential breach, high risk). Both demanded the same 8 engineers full-time.',
 'I needed to prioritize between two high-stakes initiatives with competing resource demands, making a defensible decision that balanced business value and risk while ensuring both projects succeeded.',
 'I created a quantitative framework evaluating financial impact (50M dollars vs 200M dollars plus potential costs), timeline flexibility, resource dependencies. I proposed 5 engineers to Prime Day, 3 to security, plus 400K dollars security consulting firm. I personally reviewed both daily.',
 'Prime Day launched on time generating 50M dollars revenue. Security vulnerabilities patched 5 days ahead of deadline with zero incidents. My director adopted the prioritization framework org-wide as the standard for competing initiative decisions.',
 5, 5, 5, 5, 5.0, 'senior', true),

('technical',
 'Tell me about a technical architecture decision you made',
 'At Google, I was TPM for search indexing processing 100M documents daily. Choice: Kafka plus Spark streaming (real-time, complex ops) vs BigQuery batch (4-hour latency, simpler). I ran load tests: Kafka 2M events per second with 50ms p99, BigQuery 3-hour processing but 40K dollars less monthly. I facilitated architecture reviews, analyzed team expertise (2 Spark vs 8 BigQuery experts), validated requirements (product said within 6 hours acceptable). Tradeoff: streaming superior but required hiring 2 SREs (400K dollars annually) and 6 months to build, batch met requirements with existing team in 6 weeks. I recommended batch with future upgrade option. We shipped in 7 weeks, after 8 months product confirmed 4-hour latency was better than needed. We avoided 800K dollars annual costs while meeting all requirements.',
 'At Google, I was TPM for search indexing processing 100M documents daily. Choice between Kafka plus Spark streaming (real-time but complex) and BigQuery batch (4-hour latency but simpler).',
 'I needed to make a defensible technical architecture decision balancing performance requirements, operational complexity, team expertise, cost, and time-to-market.',
 'I created evaluation framework and ran comparative load tests. I facilitated architecture reviews with 3 senior engineers, analyzed team expertise (2 Spark vs 8 BigQuery experts), validated business requirements (within 6 hours acceptable). Analysis showed streaming required hiring 2 SREs (400K dollars annually) and 6 months, batch met requirements with existing team in 6 weeks.',
 'I recommended batch processing. We shipped in 7 weeks within budget. After 8 months, product confirmed 4-hour latency exceeded their needs. We avoided 800K dollars annual costs while fully meeting requirements. The evaluation framework became our standard for build-vs-buy decisions.',
 5, 5, 5, 5, 5.0, 'senior', true),

('technical',
 'Describe how you addressed technical debt while balancing features',
 'At Microsoft, our 15-year-old payment monolith caused 40 percent of incidents and 3x slower features (6 weeks vs 2). Product leadership resisted tech debt work. I quantified: 2.1M dollars annually in opportunity cost plus 800K dollars in incident response. I proposed 20 percent rule: 1 week per sprint for tech debt, prioritized by incident impact. I created scored backlog and committed to tracking metrics weekly. Over 6 months, we refactored into 8 microservices (1 per sprint, maintaining features). Feature velocity increased 2.1x (6 weeks to 2.8 weeks), incidents dropped 78 percent (40 per quarter to 9), customer satisfaction improved 12 points. Product leadership became advocates, 20 percent rule adopted across entire Azure org.',
 'At Microsoft, our 15-year-old monolithic payment code caused 40 percent of production incidents and made feature development 3x slower (6 weeks instead of 2 weeks). Product leadership resisted technical debt work.',
 'I needed to address critical technical debt impacting both reliability and feature velocity, while maintaining feature delivery commitments and gaining buy-in from resistant product leadership.',
 'I quantified the problem at 2.1M dollars annual opportunity cost plus 800K dollars in incident response. I proposed the 20 percent rule (1 week per sprint for tech debt), created prioritized backlog scored by incident frequency and feature impact, committed to weekly metrics tracking. Over 6 months, we refactored the monolith into 8 microservices while maintaining feature delivery.',
 'Feature velocity increased 2.1x (6 weeks to 2.8 weeks average). Production incidents dropped 78 percent (40 per quarter to 9). Customer satisfaction improved by 12 points. Product leadership became advocates, and the 20 percent rule was adopted across the entire Azure organization.',
 5, 5, 5, 5, 5.0, 'senior', true);